raph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ gets more and more negative without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nThis does not give us a horizontal asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507474 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1d1712fb97a1a508a5603e055218a863eb314310a8af74cf6344a3cd7ab50731.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty $ and $f(x)\\to b$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6b3e916-f86a-5464-9d2a-5e30022c5b56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507474 #edb/ref 1074 "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $0.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ becomes more and more negative without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507492 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c0/c0da20fd092718bf477d9b00ee86f953c72c130e7da20ebbe9ae0fdb47705102.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty $ and $f(x)\\to b$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfc547ca-ecd5-5977-a221-c751ecb9addf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507492 #edb/ref 1074 "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507527 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f22c95e94846c80b0e1f6c0dc340da701f601a50a9a8908cde78d57825f38c89.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8c70775-4967-58d2-b21e-4833444c9363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507527 #edb/ref 1074 "On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a horizontal asymptote at $y=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
